<!--
  Detection: Multi-Stage Credential Access Chain (correlation rule)
  MITRE ATT&CK: T1055 -> T1003.001 -> T1059.001

  This is the flagship rule of the repo: instead of alerting on isolated
  atomic events, it correlates three independent detections
  (process injection -> LSASS memory access -> suspicious PowerShell)
  into one high-confidence, host-scoped incident using <if_matched_sid>.

  Design note on <if_matched_sid> + <if_sid> together: <if_sid> requires
  the CURRENT log to itself match that rule's chain; <if_matched_sid>
  requires the referenced rule to have fired previously, on ANY log.
  Combining them (as below) means: "this specific incoming event
  independently matches rule X, AND rule Y already fired at some earlier
  point" - i.e. "B happened, and A happened before it".

  KNOWN ENGINE LIMITATION, discovered via two rounds of real CI failure -
  NOT a design choice, read before changing this rule: a single-occurrence,
  time-bounded "A then B within N seconds" chain is not cleanly
  expressible in Wazuh's rule engine.

    Round 1: shipped with `timeframe="300"` and no `frequency`, believing
    `timeframe` alone meant "at least once in the window" (that's what
    the prose in the official docs implies). A negative test - the same
    chain with a 10-minute gap between stages - proved that wrong:
    100941 fired anyway. Root cause: `timeframe` on an `if_matched_sid`
    rule is silently ignored unless `frequency` is ALSO set - the docs
    say the two must be used together, the engine doesn't enforce that
    pairing, so a rule shipped with `timeframe` alone does nothing with
    it. Independently confirmed upstream: wazuh/wazuh#7929.

    Round 2: tried adding `frequency="1"` (matched "at least once") to
    make the pairing valid. `wazuh-manager` refused to load the file at
    all: "ERROR: rules_op: Invalid frequency: 1. Must be higher than 1
    and lower than 10000." `frequency` counts occurrences of the
    referenced rule and Wazuh requires it to be >= 2 - i.e. this
    mechanism is built for volumetric correlation ("N times in T
    seconds"), not "exactly once, within T seconds". There is no valid
    `frequency` value that means "one occurrence" here.

  Conclusion, and what this rule actually guarantees: `if_matched_sid`
  without frequency/timeframe means "the referenced rule has fired at
  any point during this analysisd session" - unbounded, not a 5-minute
  window. That's a real, useful signal (injection happened, THEN this
  LSASS access happened, in that order, on this host) - just a weaker
  guarantee than "within 5 minutes" would be. `timeframe`/`frequency` are
  deliberately absent below rather than present-but-silently-inert.
  tests/samples/100940_chain_sequence_timeout.json (10-minute gap) is
  kept as a regression test for this exact behavior - it asserts the
  chain rule DOES still fire, documenting what's actually guaranteed
  instead of a bound that was never real. See "Future work" in
  docs/detections/100940-credential-access-chain.md for how to get a
  real bounded window (query-time correlation, not the real-time rule
  engine).
-->
<group name="local,credential_access,windows,sysmon,powershell,">

  <!-- Stage 2: injection (100921) already seen (at any point in this
       session - see the header comment on why there's no time bound),
       THIS event is a dumping-consistent LSASS access (100912), same host -->
  <rule id="100941" level="13">
    <if_sid>100912</if_sid>
    <if_matched_sid>100921</if_matched_sid>
    <description>Credential access chain: process injection (rule 100921) followed by LSASS memory access (rule 100912) on the same host - high-confidence credential dumping</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1055</id>
      <id>T1003.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access_chain,attack_chain,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Stage 3: the chain above already fired, THIS event is a suspicious
       PowerShell scriptblock (100931, e.g. exfil/C2 staging) -->
  <rule id="100942" level="15">
    <if_sid>100931</if_sid>
    <if_matched_sid>100941</if_matched_sid>
    <description>Full credential access + staging chain: injection -> LSASS dump -> suspicious PowerShell on the same host - treat as active incident</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1055</id>
      <id>T1003.001</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access_chain,attack_chain,incident,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
